WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:16.110
What would happen if every microbe in the
world just...disappeared.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.110
Could we live?

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:18.930
Could anything live?

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.280
And for how long?

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.420
First, let’s talk about mass extinctions.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.220
The first microbes on this planet appeared
billions of years ago.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:36.000
In the long history of life since then, researchers
have estimated using fossil evidence that

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.250
there have been five major mass extinctions.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:46.090
These extinctions were catastrophic mixes
of geological, biological, and of course on

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:52.970
at least one occasion astronomical issues
that, over the often protracted period they

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:58.050
occurred, killed off a large percentage of
extant species.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:02.970
The end-Permian extinction, which is considered
to be the largest mass extinction, occurred

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:09.560
over 252 million years ago, the result of
volcanic activity that produced greenhouse

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.740
gases and warmed the planet.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:18.570
But more recently, researchers have identified
another potential culprit: microbes.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:26.440
Specifically: methanogens, a type of methane-producing
archaea whose numbers may have increased as

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:32.720
they fed on dying plants and animals, creating
even more methane that warmed the planet further,

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.800
killing more organisms, producing more food for methanogens

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:44.300
A classic positive feedback loop that changed
the planet too fast for many species to cope with.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:52.640
Now, this idea that microbes may have been
involved in the largest mass extinction is

00:01:52.640 --> 00:02:00.200
a hypothesis, rooted in scientific observation
but still not proven but, as we look at what

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:06.740
a world without microbes might be like, remember
that they may have, at least once, done living

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.479
systems a good deal of damage all on their
own.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:15.330
But a world without them would do far
more damage.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:20.319
With this thought and perhaps our own biases
in mind, the collective scientific understanding

00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:24.269
of microbes feels a little bit like being
in love.

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:28.650
It wasn’t long ago that we didn’t know
they existed.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:32.500
But now, it hard to imagine a world
without them.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:39.200
Now, this has not stopped scientists
from trying to imagine such a world.

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:48.299
In 2014, Jack A. Gilbert and Josh D. Neufeld
published a paper in PLOS Biology called Life

00:02:48.299 --> 00:02:55.090
in a World without Microbes, envisioning a
hypothetical world where in a single moment,

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:58.529
all microbes vanish.

00:02:58.529 --> 00:03:02.980
Their thought experiment uses what we’ve
learned of microbes to take on a belief put

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:10.849
forth by Louis Pasteur, that, “Life would
not long remain possible in the absence of microbes.”

00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:17.420
and that may have been the case then, but we have more technology now.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.360
There are many ways to explore
this hypothetical world without microbes,

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:22.660
and in their work, Gilbert

00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:27.620
and Neufeld consider what we might broadly
group into two questions.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:31.859
What would an absence of microbes do to the
world around us?

00:03:31.859 --> 00:03:36.760
And what would an absence of microbes do to the
world inside of us?

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:40.499
Let’s start with that second question: the
internal one.

00:03:40.499 --> 00:03:46.129
If all microbes, single-celled and multi-celled,
were to vanish, there would be a large number

00:03:46.129 --> 00:03:49.489
of diseases that would just cease to exist.

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:55.669
Now, in this hypothetical, mitochondria and similar
endosymbiotic prokaryotes that have long been

00:03:55.669 --> 00:04:01.950
integrated into multicellular, eukaryotic
bodies such as our own, are treated not as

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.489
microbes, but as part of our bodies.

00:04:04.489 --> 00:04:11.199
If all mitochondria disappeared, do not fear,
you would be dead that very instant.

00:04:11.199 --> 00:04:16.989
But even without endosymbionts that leaves
many, many microbes inside of us, particularly

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:21.889
bacteria in our gut that aid in digestion
and metabolism.

00:04:21.889 --> 00:04:26.400
It’s unlikely that we’ll ever know for
sure what would happen to our bodies if all

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.000
of those microbial residents were just suddenly evicted.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.530
The examples we can take from various
types of germ-free animals that have been

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:41.920
raised since the end of the 19th century suggests
that life, at least inside of us, will still

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:47.070
go on—it’s just not clear what that life
would look like for us.

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:51.531
We would likely have to rely on synthetic
nutrients to compensate for changes in how

00:04:51.531 --> 00:04:53.700
our body processes food.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:57.100
Meanwhile, our own physiology might change.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.580
We would, for example, be smaller,

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.740
having access to fewer nutrients.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:05.140
leaving us to wonder what of our body belonged

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:07.700
just to us after all.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:13.220
But of course there are many, many microbes, in fact the vast majority of them,

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:17.590
existing outside of our bodies as, and a hypothetical microbial

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:22.720
banishment would create some clear challenges.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.070
You might think that, without the work of
diatoms and other photosynthetic microbes,

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:32.460
we’d be at a risk of dying from a lack of
oxygen pretty quickly

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.110
And yes, it would be an eventual problem.

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:42.000
But it turns out there is enough atmospheric
oxygen to sustain life for a pretty long time.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.160
Gilbert and Neufeld note this as more of a
centuries-long back-up plan

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.120
while we can maybe figure something else out.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:54.760
But, if we're being honest with ourselves, we probably wouldn't make it that long.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:57.060
There are larger problems.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:01.760
Some bacteria, like Nostoc, convert diatomic
nitrogen in the atmosphere

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.930
into other nitrogen-containing

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:07.570
compounds that plants can actually use.

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:13.920
Their disappearance would severely reduce
photosynthesis, and yes, limit the further

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:20.340
release of oxygen into the atmosphere, but also,
it would just make it much harder to grow plants

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:24.080
which, you will remember, we need for food.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:31.350
Meanwhile, macroscopic decomposers would have
to take on much more of the burden of converting

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:35.150
death into new ingredients for life.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:40.480
And we would also feel the loss of other cycles
that rely on microbes, like the processes

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:44.500
that move phosphorous across different layers
of life.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:51.070
The interlocking nature of these microbe-based
processes within their environments mirrors

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:58.010
our relatively new understanding of how our
microbiome dictates the interplay between

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:00.080
microbes and our bodies.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:06.720
In fact, scientists have begun studying what
they call the Earth Microbiome.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:12.980
not just a catalogue of the diversity of microbes
across the planet, but a broader understanding of

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:21.040
how the microbial composition is its own dynamic
thing that shapes and responds to the world around it.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:26.410
There is an ongoing conversation about the
possibility that we are somewhere in the midst

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:32.600
of a sixth extinction event driven primarily
by our own human actions.

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:37.730
The characteristics of this extinction are
much more readily observable on animals and plants

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:42.921
than on microbes, but they are an important
piece of that extinction puzzle—both in

00:07:42.921 --> 00:07:47.320
terms of their future and our own.

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:53.690
Many microbes, after all, seem readily capable
of survival—like the trusty tardigrade,

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:59.639
the mascot of all microbes who do what we
would all love to do when conditions are bad,

00:07:59.639 --> 00:08:02.780
they curl up in a protective cyst and lie dormant.

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:07.751
The question, if that really is what it will
to take to survive, what will the world

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:09.740
look like when they wake up.

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:19.040
At the end of “Life in a World Without Microbes,”
Gilbert and Neufeld describe a world where

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:25.880
nutrients and resources become increasingly
scarce--a world where we would need to rapidly

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:34.630
fill the void left by microbes with manufactured
alternatives designed to fill in cavernous cracks.

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:42.070
They argue that we might actually do okay
for a few days, and maybe a small number of

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:45.700
humans would manage to last even for centuries.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:52.000
But in the long term, for us humans at least, the
prognosis is bleak.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:58.060
But they also point out that while microbes
play a massively important role in our world

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:05.460
right now, there is a hypothetical where
other organisms adapt or evolve to reconfigure

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:08.060
those essential niches.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:13.120
Perhaps we would be gone, but life exceeds us.

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:16.440
And so does its capacity for invention.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:22.010
And then, we suppose, Earth would just have to get ready for some new species to come along,

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:28.690
develop technologies and look into the fossil
record to find what caused our mass extinction,

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:35.960
and then make an internet...and then internet
videos about us, and how weird our world was,

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:38.360
back when there were microbes.

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:48.160
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:50.260
And also thank you to all of these people,

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:52.540
our patrons on Patreon,

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:55.460
who allow us to go on these deep dives

00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:00.600
and understand more of how much we rely on these little organisms.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:03.440
So various and so delightful.

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:06.860
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes James Weiss,

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:10.280
you can check out Jam and Germs on Instagram

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:13.460
And you want to see more from us, here at Journey to the Microcosmos,

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:18.740
you can find us at YouTube.com/microcosmos

